## 2. Template Non-Type Parameters

A **non-type parameter (NTTP)** is a *value* known at compile time, not a type — e.g. an integer, an enum, a pointer/reference to something with static storage duration, or (since C++20) certain class types (literal types, including some floating-point types via `auto` NTTPs).

```cpp
template <typename T, std::size_t N>
class FixedArray {
    T data[N]; // N must be a compile-time constant
public:
    constexpr std::size_t size() const { return N; }
    T& operator[](std::size_t i) { return data[i]; }
};

int main() {
    FixedArray<int, 5> arr;   // N = 5, fixed at compile time
    arr[0] = 10;
    static_assert(FixedArray<int,5>{}.size() == 5);
}
```

Non-type parameters can also have default values and can be used for compile-time computation:

```cpp
template <int N>
struct Factorial {
    static constexpr int value = N * Factorial<N - 1>::value;
};

template <>
struct Factorial<0> {
    static constexpr int value = 1;
};

static_assert(Factorial<5>::value == 120);
```

C++20 allows `auto` as a non-type template parameter, deducing the NTTP's type:

```cpp
template <auto N>
struct Wrapper {
    static constexpr auto value = N;
};

Wrapper<10> w1;      // N deduced as int
Wrapper<'A'> w2;     // N deduced as char
```

