## 3. Template Template Parameters

A **template template parameter** is a parameter that is itself a *class template*, not a concrete type. It's used when you want to parameterize which container template (e.g. `vector` vs `list`) to use internally, while still letting the caller pick the contained type separately.

```cpp
template <typename T, template <typename, typename> class Container>
class Stack {
    Container<T, std::allocator<T>> data;
public:
    void push(const T& value) { data.push_back(value); }
    void pop() { data.pop_back(); }
    T& top() { return data.back(); }
    bool empty() const { return data.empty(); }
};

int main() {
    Stack<int, std::vector> s1;   // uses std::vector<int> internally
    Stack<int, std::deque>  s2;   // uses std::deque<int> internally
    s1.push(10);
    s2.push(20);
}
```

**Note:** the template parameter list of the template-template parameter (here `template <typename, typename> class Container`) must match the signature of the template being passed (`std::vector` actually takes `<T, Allocator>`, so we declare it that way). Since C++17, this matching is more lenient (default arguments in the passed template are considered).

