## 4. Template Arguments

**Template arguments** are the concrete values (types, constants, or templates) supplied for a template's parameters when it is used/instantiated.

### Explicit Template Arguments

You explicitly write out the argument(s) in angle brackets instead of relying on deduction.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T identity(T x) { return x; }

int main() {
    identity<double>(5);   // explicit: T = double, 5 is converted to 5.0
    identity(5);            // deduced: T = int
}
```

Explicit arguments are **required** when the compiler cannot deduce them, e.g. when the type parameter doesn't appear in the function parameter list:

```cpp
template <typename T>
T getDefault() { return T{}; }

int main() {
    int x = getDefault<int>();      // must specify T explicitly
    std::string s = getDefault<std::string>();
}
```

### Default Template Arguments

A template parameter can have a default, used when the caller doesn't supply one.

```cpp
template <typename T = int, int N = 10>
class Buffer {
    T data[N];
public:
    constexpr int size() const { return N; }
};

int main() {
    Buffer<> b1;          // T = int, N = 10  (both defaults)
    Buffer<double> b2;    // T = double, N = 10
    Buffer<double, 20> b3; // T = double, N = 20
}
```

Default template arguments also work for function templates (C++11+):

```cpp
template <typename T, typename Compare = std::less<T>>
bool isSorted(const std::vector<T>& v, Compare comp = Compare{}) {
    return std::is_sorted(v.begin(), v.end(), comp);
}
```

