## 5. Template Instantiation

**Instantiation** is the process where the compiler generates actual code (a concrete function/class) from a template, substituting the template parameters with real arguments. This happens only when the template is actually *used* — this is the "as-if used" or **implicit instantiation** rule.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T square(T x) { return x * x; }

int main() {
    square(5);      // implicit instantiation: compiler generates square<int>
    square(2.5);    // implicit instantiation: compiler generates square<double>
    // A template that's never called generates NO code at all.
}
```

### Explicit Instantiation

You can force the compiler to instantiate a template even without a call site — useful for reducing compile times across translation units or for pre-compiling template code into a library.

```cpp
template <typename T>
T square(T x) { return x * x; }

// Explicit instantiation definition — forces code generation here:
template int square<int>(int);
template double square<double>(double);
```

### Explicit Instantiation Declaration (`extern template`, C++11)

Tells the compiler *not* to instantiate the template in this translation unit (it's assumed to be instantiated elsewhere), avoiding duplicate work across multiple `.cpp` files.

```cpp
// header.h
extern template class std::vector<int>;   // declaration only, don't instantiate here

// one .cpp file
template class std::vector<int>;          // actual instantiation happens here
```

### Two-Phase Lookup / Compilation Model

Templates are compiled in **two phases**:
1. At definition time — syntax and non-dependent names are checked.
2. At instantiation time — dependent names (those depending on template parameters) are resolved and type-checked with the actual arguments.

This is why template definitions typically live in headers: the compiler needs the full definition available at every point of instantiation (unless explicit instantiation is used).

