## 6. Function Templates

A **function template** defines a family of functions parameterized by type(s) and/or value(s).

```cpp
template <typename T>
void swapValues(T& a, T& b) {
    T temp = a;
    a = b;
    b = temp;
}

int main() {
    int x = 1, y = 2;
    swapValues(x, y);   // instantiates swapValues<int>

    std::string s1 = "hi", s2 = "bye";
    swapValues(s1, s2);  // instantiates swapValues<std::string>
}
```

### Function Template Argument Deduction

The compiler infers template arguments from the types of the function call arguments — you usually don't need to write `<T>` explicitly.

```cpp
template <typename T>
void print(T value) { std::cout << value << "\n"; }

print(42);        // T deduced as int
print(3.14);       // T deduced as double
print("hello");    // T deduced as const char*
```

**Deduction rules with references:**

```cpp
template <typename T>
void f(T param);          // param is a copy — T deduced by decaying (removes const/ref)

template <typename T>
void g(T& param);         // param is a reference — const-ness is preserved in T

template <typename T>
void h(T&& param);        // "universal/forwarding reference" — T deduced differently
                            // for lvalues (T = U&) vs rvalues (T = U)

int main() {
    const int ci = 10;
    f(ci);   // T = int         (const stripped, param is a copy)
    g(ci);   // T = const int   (reference preserves const)

    int x = 5;
    h(x);        // lvalue passed  -> T = int&,  param type = int&
    h(10);       // rvalue passed  -> T = int,   param type = int&&
}
```

Deduction can also fail when there's ambiguity — e.g. mismatched types for the same parameter used twice:

```cpp
template <typename T>
T addSame(T a, T b) { return a + b; }

addSame(3, 4);       // OK, T = int
// addSame(3, 4.5);  // ERROR: T deduced as int from first arg, double from second — conflict
addSame<double>(3, 4.5); // OK — explicit argument resolves ambiguity
```

### Function Template Overloading

Multiple function templates (or a template alongside a plain, non-template function) can share the same name; overload resolution picks the best match, with **non-template functions preferred over templates when both match equally well**.

```cpp
template <typename T>
void process(T value) {
    std::cout << "Generic template: " << value << "\n";
}

template <typename T>
void process(T* ptr) {
    std::cout << "Pointer specialization: " << *ptr << "\n";
}

void process(int value) {   // plain (non-template) overload
    std::cout << "Non-template int overload: " << value << "\n";
}

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    process(x);     // calls the non-template overload (preferred over template)
    process(&x);     // calls process<T*> — pointer overload is more specialized
    process(3.14);   // calls process<T> generic template
}
```

**Overload resolution order (simplified):**
1. Exact match (non-template preferred over template if otherwise tied)
2. Best template match (more specialized template preferred, e.g. `T*` over `T`)
3. Match requiring implicit conversions

