## 8. Class Template Argument Deduction (CTAD, C++17)

**CTAD** lets you construct an object of a class template without explicitly specifying template arguments — the compiler deduces them from the constructor arguments, similar to function template deduction.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Box {
    T value;
public:
    Box(T v) : value(v) {}
};

int main() {
    Box b1(42);           // CTAD: deduces Box<int>       (pre-C++17: Box<int> b1(42); was required)
    Box b2(3.14);          // CTAD: deduces Box<double>
    Box b3("hello");       // CTAD: deduces Box<const char*>

    std::vector v{1, 2, 3};       // CTAD: std::vector<int>
    std::pair p(1, "one");        // CTAD: std::pair<int, const char*>
}
```

### User-Defined Deduction Guides

Sometimes the implicit deduction (from constructors) isn't what you want; you can supply a **deduction guide** to steer CTAD.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Box {
    T value;
public:
    Box(T v) : value(v) {}
};

// Deduction guide: force any Box constructed from a char* to become Box<std::string>
Box(const char*) -> Box<std::string>;

int main() {
    Box b("hello");  // Without the guide, this would deduce Box<const char*>.
                       // With the guide, it deduces Box<std::string>.
}
```

`std::vector`'s iterator-pair constructor is a classic real-world example needing a guide:

```cpp
template <typename Iterator>
std::vector(Iterator, Iterator)
    -> std::vector<typename std::iterator_traits<Iterator>::value_type>;
```

