## 9. Member Templates

A **member template** is a template member function (or nested template class) inside a regular class or class template. It allows a member to be generic independently of the enclosing class's own template parameters.

```cpp
class Printer {
public:
    template <typename T>          // member function template
    void print(const T& value) {
        std::cout << value << "\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Printer p;
    p.print(42);        // instantiates print<int>
    p.print("hello");   // instantiates print<const char*>
}
```

Member templates are especially common as **converting constructors** in class templates, e.g. to allow `Box<Derived>` to convert to `Box<Base>`:

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Box {
    T value;
public:
    Box(T v) : value(v) {}

    template <typename U>          // member template constructor
    Box(const Box<U>& other) : value(other.get()) {}

    T get() const { return value; }
};

struct Base {};
struct Derived : Base {};

int main() {
    Box<Derived*> derivedBox(new Derived());
    Box<Base*> baseBox(derivedBox);   // uses the member template converting constructor
}
```

Nested member class templates are also possible:

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Outer {
public:
    template <typename U>
    class Inner {
        T outerVal;
        U innerVal;
    public:
        Inner(T o, U i) : outerVal(o), innerVal(i) {}
    };
};

Outer<int>::Inner<double> obj(1, 2.5);
```

