## 10. Template Specialization

**Specialization** lets you provide a custom implementation of a template for specific argument(s), overriding the generic (primary) template.

### Explicit / Full Specialization

**All** template parameters are fixed to concrete values/types — you provide a completely separate implementation for one exact combination.

```cpp
// Primary (generic) template
template <typename T>
class TypeName {
public:
    static std::string name() { return "unknown"; }
};

// Full specialization for int
template <>
class TypeName<int> {
public:
    static std::string name() { return "int"; }
};

// Full specialization for double
template <>
class TypeName<double> {
public:
    static std::string name() { return "double"; }
};

int main() {
    std::cout << TypeName<int>::name() << "\n";     // "int"
    std::cout << TypeName<double>::name() << "\n";   // "double"
    std::cout << TypeName<char>::name() << "\n";     // "unknown" (falls back to primary)
}
```

Function templates can be fully specialized too (though overloading is often preferred in practice):

```cpp
template <typename T>
bool isEqual(T a, T b) { return a == b; }

template <>
bool isEqual<const char*>(const char* a, const char* b) {
    return std::strcmp(a, b) == 0;   // special logic for C-strings
}
```

### Partial Specialization

**Only some** of the template parameters are fixed, or a pattern (like "any pointer type" or "any pair of two identical types") is specified instead of one exact type. **Partial specialization is only allowed for class templates**, not function templates (functions use overloading instead to achieve a similar effect).

```cpp
// Primary template
template <typename T, typename U>
class Pair {
public:
    static std::string describe() { return "generic pair"; }
};

// Partial specialization: both types are the same
template <typename T>
class Pair<T, T> {
public:
    static std::string describe() { return "pair of identical types"; }
};

// Partial specialization: second type is a pointer
template <typename T, typename U>
class Pair<T, U*> {
public:
    static std::string describe() { return "pair with pointer as second type"; }
};

int main() {
    std::cout << Pair<int, double>::describe() << "\n";  // "generic pair"
    std::cout << Pair<int, int>::describe() << "\n";      // "pair of identical types"
    std::cout << Pair<int, double*>::describe() << "\n";  // "pair with pointer as second type"
}
```

A very common real-world use of partial specialization is trait detection:

```cpp
template <typename T>
struct IsPointer {
    static constexpr bool value = false;
};

template <typename T>
struct IsPointer<T*> {   // partial specialization: matches any pointer type
    static constexpr bool value = true;
};

static_assert(IsPointer<int>::value == false);
static_assert(IsPointer<int*>::value == true);
```

