## 11. SFINAE

**SFINAE** = "Substitution Failure Is Not An Error." When substituting template arguments produces an invalid type/expression in the *immediate context*, the compiler silently removes that overload/specialization from the candidate set instead of raising a hard error — it just tries the next candidate.

### Classic SFINAE with `std::enable_if`

```cpp
#include <type_traits>

// Only participates in overload resolution if T is an integral type
template <typename T, typename std::enable_if<std::is_integral<T>::value, int>::type = 0>
void process(T value) {
    std::cout << "Integral: " << value << "\n";
}

// Only participates if T is a floating-point type
template <typename T, typename std::enable_if<std::is_floating_point<T>::value, int>::type = 0>
void process(T value) {
    std::cout << "Floating point: " << value << "\n";
}

int main() {
    process(5);      // calls the integral overload
    process(3.14);    // calls the floating-point overload
    // process("hi"); // ERROR: no matching overload — SFINAE eliminated both candidates
}
```

### SFINAE on the Return Type

```cpp
template <typename T>
typename std::enable_if<std::is_integral<T>::value, T>::type
doubleIt(T x) {
    return x * 2;
}
```

### SFINAE via `decltype` — Detecting Whether an Expression is Valid

A common pattern: detect at compile time whether a type has a certain member function.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class HasToString {
    template <typename U>
    static auto test(int) -> decltype(std::declval<U>().toString(), std::true_type{});

    template <typename U>
    static std::false_type test(...);   // fallback (matches anything, lowest priority)

public:
    static constexpr bool value = decltype(test<T>(0))::value;
};

struct WithToString { std::string toString() { return "hi"; } };
struct WithoutToString {};

static_assert(HasToString<WithToString>::value == true);
static_assert(HasToString<WithoutToString>::value == false);
```

### Modern Alternative: `if constexpr` and Concepts (C++17/C++20)

SFINAE is powerful but famously hard to read. Since C++17, `if constexpr` (see section 15) removes the need for SFINAE in many everyday cases, and C++20 **concepts** replace `enable_if`-based constraints with much more readable syntax:

```cpp
// C++20 concept-based constraint — cleaner alternative to SFINAE
template <typename T>
concept Integral = std::is_integral_v<T>;

template <Integral T>
void process(T value) {
    std::cout << "Integral: " << value << "\n";
}
```

