## 12. Variadic Templates

**Variadic templates** (C++11) accept an arbitrary number of template arguments via a **parameter pack**, denoted with `...`.

```cpp
template <typename... Args>       // Args is a template parameter pack
void printAll(Args... args) {      // args is a function parameter pack
    // recursive expansion is needed pre-C++17 (see below), C++17 uses fold expressions
}
```

### Classic Recursive Expansion (pre-C++17 style)

```cpp
// Base case: no arguments left
void printAll() {
    std::cout << "\n";
}

// Recursive case: peel off the first argument, recurse on the rest
template <typename T, typename... Rest>
void printAll(T first, Rest... rest) {
    std::cout << first << " ";
    printAll(rest...);   // expands the pack by one less argument each call
}

int main() {
    printAll(1, 2.5, "hello", 'c');  // prints: 1 2.5 hello c
}
```

### `sizeof...` — Counting Pack Elements

```cpp
template <typename... Args>
void countArgs(Args... args) {
    std::cout << "Number of arguments: " << sizeof...(Args) << "\n";
    // or equivalently: sizeof...(args)
}

countArgs(1, 2, 3);       // 3
countArgs("a", 1.5);      // 2
```

### Variadic Class Templates

```cpp
template <typename... Types>
class Tuple;   // forward declaration, like std::tuple

// Real-world example: std::tuple itself is a variadic class template
std::tuple<int, double, std::string> t(1, 2.5, "hi");
```

### Variadic Templates with Perfect Forwarding (very common pattern — used in `emplace_back`)

```cpp
template <typename T, typename... Args>
T* makeOnHeap(Args&&... args) {
    return new T(std::forward<Args>(args)...);   // forwards each argument, preserving value category
}

struct Point { Point(int x, int y) {} };

Point* p = makeOnHeap<Point>(3, 4);   // constructs Point(3, 4) directly, forwarding args
```

