## 13. Perfect Forwarding

**Perfect forwarding** preserves the value category (lvalue/rvalue) and const-ness of a function argument when passing it on to another function — avoiding unnecessary copies and preserving move semantics. It relies on **universal (forwarding) references** (`T&&` where `T` is a deduced template parameter) combined with `std::forward`.

### The Problem Without Perfect Forwarding

```cpp
template <typename T>
void wrapper(T param) {          // always makes a copy
    target(param);                // always passes an lvalue, even if wrapper() received an rvalue
}
```

### The Solution

```cpp
template <typename T>
void wrapper(T&& param) {              // universal reference: binds to both lvalues and rvalues
    target(std::forward<T>(param));     // forwards param preserving its original value category
}

void target(const std::string& s) { std::cout << "lvalue overload: " << s << "\n"; }
void target(std::string&& s)      { std::cout << "rvalue overload: " << s << "\n"; }

int main() {
    std::string s = "hello";
    wrapper(s);              // s is an lvalue -> calls target(const std::string&)
    wrapper(std::string("temp")); // rvalue -> calls target(std::string&&) — no unnecessary copy
}
```

### How `std::forward` Works

- If `T` was deduced as `U&` (lvalue passed in), `std::forward<T>(param)` produces an lvalue.
- If `T` was deduced as `U` (rvalue passed in), `std::forward<T>(param)` produces an rvalue (via `static_cast<T&&>`).

### Real-World Example: `emplace_back`

```cpp
template <typename T>
class MyVector {
    // ...
public:
    template <typename... Args>
    void emplace_back(Args&&... args) {
        // constructs T in-place, forwarding each argument perfectly
        new (allocate_slot()) T(std::forward<Args>(args)...);
    }
};
```

**Important distinction:** `T&&` is only a "universal/forwarding reference" when `T` is a template parameter deduced *in that same function call*. In a non-deduced context (e.g. `std::vector<T>&&` inside a class template already instantiated with a concrete `T`), `&&` is just an ordinary rvalue reference.

