## 16. Variable Templates

**Variable templates** (C++14) let you define a family of variables/constants parameterized by type or value, without wrapping them in a class (`::value` boilerplate).

```cpp
template <typename T>
constexpr T pi = T(3.1415926535897932385);

int main() {
    std::cout << pi<float> << "\n";    // pi as a float
    std::cout << pi<double> << "\n";    // pi as a double
}
```

### Before Variable Templates (C++11 style, verbose)

```cpp
template <typename T>
struct Pi {
    static constexpr T value = T(3.14159265358979323846);
};

Pi<double>::value;   // more verbose access
```

### Real-World Example: the `_v` Suffix Convention in `<type_traits>`

Most `<type_traits>` traits ending in `_v` (e.g. `std::is_integral_v<T>`) are variable templates defined in terms of the corresponding `::value` trait struct:

```cpp
template <typename T>
inline constexpr bool is_integral_v = std::is_integral<T>::value;

// Usage:
static_assert(std::is_integral_v<int>);      // cleaner than std::is_integral<int>::value
```

Variable templates can also depend on non-type parameters:

```cpp
template <int N>
constexpr int square = N * N;

static_assert(square<5> == 25);
```

