## 17. Alias Templates

An **alias template** (C++11) defines a template that is itself an alias for another type — essentially a "typedef with template parameters" (which plain `typedef` cannot express).

```cpp
template <typename T>
using Vec = std::vector<T>;

int main() {
    Vec<int> v = {1, 2, 3};   // same as std::vector<int>
}
```

### Why Not `typedef`? — `typedef` Cannot Be Templated

```cpp
// This does NOT work — typedef cannot have template parameters:
// template <typename T>
// typedef std::vector<T> Vec;   // ERROR

// The alias template equivalent works fine:
template <typename T>
using Vec = std::vector<T>;      // OK
```

### Partially Fixing Some Parameters

A very common use case: fixing one template parameter of a multi-parameter template while leaving another generic.

```cpp
template <typename Key, typename Value>
using StringMap = std::map<std::string, Value>;   // Key fixed to std::string

StringMap<void, int> m;   // effectively std::map<std::string, int>
                            // (the unused "Key" placeholder is a minor awkwardness here;
                            //  a cleaner version omits the unused parameter entirely)

template <typename Value>
using StringKeyMap = std::map<std::string, Value>;   // cleaner: only Value is generic

StringKeyMap<int> m2;   // std::map<std::string, int>
```

### Alias Templates for Function Pointer / Complex Types

```cpp
template <typename R, typename... Args>
using FuncPtr = R(*)(Args...);

FuncPtr<int, double, double> fp;   // equivalent to: int (*fp)(double, double);
```

### Alias Templates Combined with `type_traits` (`_t` Suffix Convention)

```cpp
template <typename T>
using remove_const_t = typename std::remove_const<T>::type;   // this is how std:: defines its _t aliases

remove_const_t<const int> x = 5;   // x has type int
```
